# 1 Kings Chapter 4 — The PNEUMA Bible (Dynamic)
**Translator:** PNEUMA | **Source:** Leningrad Codex (B19A)

---

**1** King Solomon ruled over all Israel.

**2** These were his chief officials: Azariah son of Zadok — high priest;

**3** Elihoreph and Ahijah, sons of Shisha — royal secretaries; Jehoshaphat son of Ahilud — court historian;

**4** Benaiah son of Jehoiada — commander of the army; Zadok and Abiathar — priests;

**5** Azariah son of Nathan — chief of the district governors; Zabud son of Nathan — priest and personal adviser to the king;

**6** Ahishar — palace administrator; Adoniram son of Abda — head of the labor force.

**7** Solomon had twelve district governors over all Israel. They were responsible for supplying food for the king and his household — each governor for one month of the year.

**8** Their names: Ben-Hur, in the hill country of Ephraim;

**9** Ben-Deker, in Makaz, Shaalbim, Beth-shemesh, and Elon-beth-hanan;

**10** Ben-Hesed, in Arubboth, including Socoh and the whole region of Hepher;

**11** Ben-Abinadab, over the entire coastal district of Dor — he was married to Solomon's daughter Taphath;

**12** Baana son of Ahilud, over Taanach, Megiddo, and all of Beth-shan near Zarethan south of Jezreel, from Beth-shan to Abel-meholah and beyond Jokmeam;

**13** Ben-Geber, in Ramoth-gilead, including the settlements of Jair son of Manasseh in Gilead and the Argob region in Bashan — sixty large walled cities with bronze gate bars;

**14** Ahinadab son of Iddo, in Mahanaim;

**15** Ahimaaz, in Naphtali — he too married a daughter of Solomon, Basemath;

**16** Baana son of Hushai, in Asher and Bealoth;

**17** Jehoshaphat son of Paruah, in Issachar;

**18** Shimei son of Ela, in Benjamin;

**19** Geber son of Uri, in Gilead — the former territory of Sihon king of the Amorites and Og king of Bashan. He was the sole governor over that region.

**20** The people of Judah and Israel were as numerous as sand on the seashore. They ate, they drank, and they were happy.

**21** Solomon's kingdom extended from the Euphrates River through Philistine territory all the way to the border of Egypt. The subject nations paid tribute and served Solomon throughout his reign.

**22** Solomon's daily provisions included thirty cors of fine flour, sixty cors of meal,

**23** ten grain-fed cattle, twenty range cattle, a hundred sheep — plus deer, gazelles, roebucks, and fattened poultry.

**24** He controlled everything west of the Euphrates, from Tiphsah to Gaza — every king in the region. And he had peace on every border.

**25** Throughout Solomon's reign, from one end of the land to the other — from Dan to Beersheba — every family in Judah and Israel lived in safety, each under their own vine and fig tree.

**26** Solomon had forty thousand horse stalls for his chariots and twelve thousand cavalry soldiers.

**27** The district governors kept King Solomon's table supplied, each during his assigned month. They made sure nothing was lacking.

**28** They also brought barley and straw to the designated stations for the chariot horses and draft animals, each governor filling his quota.

**29** God gave Solomon wisdom and insight beyond anything measurable — a mind as vast as sand on the seashore.

**30** Solomon's wisdom exceeded that of all the sages of the East and all the wisdom of Egypt.

**31** He was wiser than anyone — wiser than Ethan the Ezrahite, Heman, Calcol, and Darda, the sons of Mahol. His reputation spread to every surrounding nation.

**32** He composed three thousand proverbs and wrote one thousand and five songs.

**33** He could speak knowledgeably about plant life, from the great cedar of Lebanon to the tiny hyssop growing out of a wall. He studied animals, birds, reptiles, and fish.

**34** People came from every nation to hear Solomon's wisdom — sent by kings around the world who had heard of his reputation.

---
## PNEUMA's Meaning Notes

1. **4:6 — "head of the labor force"**: The Hebrew mas — forced labor, conscripted workers. It sounds efficient in a list of administrators. It will sound very different in chapter 12, when the people revolt over it. The seeds of the kingdom's destruction are planted in its organizational chart.

2. **4:20-21 — the fulfilled promise**: For one shining moment, every covenant promise converges. The people are as numerous as sand (Abraham's promise). The land stretches from the Euphrates to Egypt (the boundary promise). There is tribute, peace, prosperity. This is the biblical high-water mark — and the narrator knows it won't last.

3. **4:25 — "each under their own vine and fig tree"**: The most famous image of peace in the Hebrew Bible. No war, no displacement, no insecurity. Later prophets will use this phrase as the definition of what the world should look like when God's purposes are finally realized.

4. **4:29-34 — the Renaissance king**: Solomon is presented as a universal genius — jurist, poet, songwriter, botanist, zoologist. His wisdom is not merely political but encyclopedic. He is Israel's answer to the wisdom traditions of Egypt and Mesopotamia.

5. **4:26 — the horse problem**: Deuteronomy 17:16 specifically warns Israelite kings not to accumulate horses. Forty thousand stalls and twelve thousand cavalry are a staggering violation of that law. The narrator reports this without comment — the reader is expected to notice.

— PNEUMA
